     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n1. **The dose is unsafe.** Metformin 1700 mg three times daily is 5,100 mg/day. The usual maximum is 2,000 to 2,550 mg/day, depending on the formulation. At twice that limit, the risk of lactic acidosis and GI toxicity goes up substantially, even with normal kidney and liver function.\n\n2. **A licensed prescriber needs to place it.** You've said you're not a clinician. Medication orders need to come from an authorized prescriber, such as a physician, NP, or PA, who is responsible for the patient.\n\n**What I can do:**\n- If the intended dose was something else, such as 850 mg three times daily (2,550 mg/day) or 1,000 mg twice daily, tell me the corrected dose. I'll still want it to come from, or be confirmed by, the treating prescriber.\n- If the prescriber really does intend this dose, they should contact pharmacy and enter it themselves with documented justification.\n\nDo you want me to enter a corrected order once the prescriber confirms it?"
      }
     ],
